{"step_index":307,"source":"SYSTEM","type":"SYSTEM_MESSAGE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:46:34Z","content":"The following is a <SYSTEM_MESSAGE> not actually sent by the user. It is provided by the system as important information to pay attention to.\n\n<SYSTEM_MESSAGE>\n[Message] timestamp=2026-09-11T18:46:16Z sender=b3ad7580-0176-458c-bebc-0ec3ed731f9e priority=MESSAGE_PRIORITY_HIGH content=Tip for `mem_ballWithin_one_of_adj` and `mem_ballWithin_one_self`:\nYou can prove them in 1 line using your existing lemmas!\n```lean\nlemma mem_ballWithin_one_of_adj {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n    (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (c u : V) (hadj : G.Adj c u) :\n    u ∈ ballWithin G 1 c := by\n  have p : G.Walk c u := SimpleGraph.Walk.cons hadj SimpleGraph.Walk.nil\n  exact mem_ballWithin_of_walk G p\n\nlemma mem_ballWithin_one_self {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n    (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (c : V) :\n    c ∈ ballWithin G 1 c :=\n  ballWithin_subset_succ G 0 c (Finset.mem_singleton_self c)\n```\nThis avoids unfolding `ballWithin` and worrying about `insert c ...`!\n</SYSTEM_MESSAGE>"}
